Optimal Solution - Use DFS to explore each island and track the maximum area by marking visited cells.
                    I run DFS from every unvisited land cell, compute the connected component size, and keep the maximum.

class Solution {
    // Tracks whether a cell has already been visited
    boolean[][] seen;
    public int maxAreaOfIsland(int[][] grid) {
        int rows = grid.length;
        int cols = grid[0].length;
        // Visited array to avoid reprocessing cells
        seen = new boolean[rows][cols];
        int maxArea = 0;
        // Traverse every cell in the grid
        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
                // Compute area of island starting at (i, j)
                maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, dfs(grid, i, j));
            }
        }
        return maxArea;
    }
    // DFS to compute the area of an island
    private int dfs(int[][] grid, int i, int j) {
        // Boundary checks + water + already visited
        if (i < 0 || j < 0 || i >= grid.length || j >= grid[0].length
                || grid[i][j] == 0 || seen[i][j]) {
            return 0;
        }
        // Mark current cell as visited
        seen[i][j] = true;
        // Area = 1 (current cell) + areas of neighbors
        return 1
                + dfs(grid, i + 1, j)   // down
                + dfs(grid, i - 1, j)   // up
                + dfs(grid, i, j + 1)   // right
                + dfs(grid, i, j - 1);  // left
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(n x m).
Space Complexity :- O(n x m).
